==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|newly}}, {{m|enm|newely}}, {{m|enm|neweliche}}, from {{inh|en|ang|nīewlīċe|t=newly}}, equivalent to {{af|en|new|-ly|id2=adverbial}}. Compare {{cog|nl|nieuwelijks}}, {{cog|de|neulich}}, {{cog|da|nylig}}, {{cog|is|nýlega}}. More at {{m|en|new}}, {{m|en|-ly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈnjuːli/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈnjʉwlɪj/|a=SSB}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-newly.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈnuli/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈnjʉːli/|a=AU!Australia,NZ}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈnjʉle/|/-lɪ/|/-li/|a=Scotland}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈnɪʊli/|a=Wales}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːli|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|new|ly}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|more}}

# Very recently/lately; in the immediate past.
#: {{ux|en|She smelled the '''[[newly]]''' budding flowers.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=3|passage=One saint's day in mid-term a certain '''newly''' appointed suffragan-bishop came to the school chapel, and there preached on “The Inner Life.”}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|freshly}}, {{l|en|recently}}; see also [[Thesaurus:recently]]

